      "command": "bun test",
      "exitCode": 0,
      "durationMs": 12815,
      "tail": "<scene-comp> defines 'onSceneAddition' — on<Event>-shaped member name(s), which collide with the elements factory's event-handler sugar. Since 1.8.0, IF the member holds a function when the creator runs, it wins: creator({ onSceneAddition: fn }) assigns it rather than attaching a 'sceneaddition' listener (tosijs#22) — and then that name can no longer carry event sugar. A member declared but left undefined/null still gets event sugar, so the meaning depends on initialisation: give it a function default, or rename to handle<Event> for a component callback / add<Event>Listener for something that registers a listener.\n\n 1068 pass\n 0 fail\n 2941 expect() calls\nRan 1068 tests across 37 files. [12.79s]"
    },
    "test:browser": {
      "command": "playwright test",
      "exitCode": 0,
      "durationMs": 180416,
      "tail": "[WebServer] $ bun bin/site.ts\n[WebServer] [105.74ms] prebuild\n[WebServer] ⚠️  The .js files tsc just emitted into dist/ have 142 relative import(s) without a file extension — Node rejects them (ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND), even though Bun and bundlers resolve them. Use `libraryBundle` instead of libraryTsconfig (tosijs-ui#169). First: configure-tjs-debug.js: ./version\n[WebServer]    If a later step replaces these files with bundles, they are not what you publish: set \"emitDeclarationOnly\": true in ./tsconfig.build.json so tsc emits only the .d.ts files.\n[WebServer] [1.86s] build\n[WebServer] [58.77ms] docs iife"
    }
